{"name":"Shaded Hammock","hex":"#859c9b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shaded","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Being in the shade, not in direct light.","Having a slight colour added (of a diagram or picture) or being slightly darkened as if in the shade."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of shade"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shaded"},{"term":"hammock","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A swinging couch or bed, usually made of netting or canvas about six feet (1.8 meters) wide, suspended by clews or cords at the ends.","A piece of land thickly wooded, and usually covered with bushes and vines."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To lie in a hammock.","To hang in a way that resembles a hammock."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hammock"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shading","blocks":[{"text":"Shading refers to the depiction of depth perception in 3D models or illustrations by varying the level of darkness. Shading tries to approximate local behavior of light on the object's surface and is not to be confused with techniques of adding shadows, such as shadow mapping or shadow volumes, which fall under global behavior of light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Flatshading01.png/330px-Flatshading01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shading"},{"title":"Hammock","blocks":[{"text":"A hammock, from Spanish hamaca, borrowed from Taíno and Arawak hamaka, is a sling made of fabric, rope, or netting, suspended between two or more points, used for swinging, sleeping, or resting. It normally consists of one or more cloth panels, or a woven network of twine or thin rope stretched with ropes between two firm anchor points such as trees or posts. Hammocks were developed by native inhabitants of the Americas for sleeping, as well as the English. Later, they were used aboard ships by sailors to enable comfort and maximize available space, by explorers or soldiers travelling in wooded regions and eventually by parents in the early 1920s for containing babies just learning to crawl. Today they are popular around the world for relaxation; they are also used as a lightweight bed on camping trips. The hammock is often seen as a symbol of summer, leisure, relaxation and simple, easy living."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Hammock11.jpg/330px-Hammock11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hammock"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Gouraudshading01.png/500px-Gouraudshading01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Gouraudshading01.png/500px-Gouraudshading01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGouraudshading01.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Phongshading01.png/500px-Phongshading01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Phongshading01.png/500px-Phongshading01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhongshading01.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Gouraud_high.gif/330px-Gouraud_high.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Gouraud_high.gif/330px-Gouraud_high.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gouraud shading, developed by Henri Gouraud in 1971, was one of the first shading techniques developed for 3D computer graphics.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGouraud_high.gif"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/Shading1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/Shading1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShading1.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Floodlight.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Floodlight.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Shading effects from a floodlight using a ray tracer","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFloodlight.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/2squares-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/2squares-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2squares-1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/2squares-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/2squares-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2squares-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Shading1.jpg/330px-Shading1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Shading1.jpg/330px-Shading1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or